Archive: m4-1.4.21.tar.xz
Origname: m4-1.4.21
Name: m4

    GNU m4 1.4.21 with the Fil-C port, carried over from 1.4.19:

    - lib/fpucw.h: under __FILC__, route the FPU control word through
      pizlonated_math.h (zmath_getcw/zmath_setcw) instead of x87 inline
      asm, which Fil-C cannot execute.
    - lib/obstack.c + lib/obstack(.in).h: obstack chunks are sized to a
      single object (minimal chunk_size; _obstack_newchunk grows the
      chunk geometrically with the object instead of adding the usual
      slack), so each object gets its own allocator-provided bounds;
      obstack_blank always calls _obstack_newchunk so a new object never
      starts inside the previous object's chunk; __PTR_ALIGN (formerly
      __BPTR_ALIGN) re-derives the aligned pointer with zmkptr so the
      result keeps a valid Fil-C capability.
    - lib/sigsegv.in.h: HAVE_STACK_OVERFLOW_RECOVERY is disabled under
      __FILC__ (1.4.21 renamed __GNU__ to __gnu_hurd__ in the condition).
    - src/macro.c: the argv pointer into the obstack is re-derived with
      zmkptr from obstack_base + offset.
    - configure: backport gnulib's serial 3 handling of
      posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir (sourceware bug 34437) directly
      into the pre-generated configure script: use ac_fn_check_decl
      (AC_CHECK_DECLS semantics) instead of the AC_CHECK_FUNCS link test,
      and replace the function via rpl_posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir
      when it is declared. Fil-C's clang predefines _POSIX_C_SOURCE
      202405L, which turns on glibc 2.44's __USE_XOPEN2K24XSI, which makes
      <spawn.h> declare posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir as a __REDIRECT
      alias of posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir_np. The AC_CHECK_FUNCS
      link test then fails (the conftest's "char f (void);" declaration
      does not inherit the redirect, and libc does not export a non-_np
      dynamic symbol), so gnulib compiled its own forwarder
      "return posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir_np (...)" - but the
      definition inherited the __REDIRECT asm label, so the forwarder
      called itself and filc trapped with a stack overflow (test-posix_spawn-chdir,
      test-posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir and test-execute.sh case 21
      all died this way). Routing all callers through
      rpl_posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir (a name that has no redirect)
      fixes it. The corresponding m4/posix_spawn_faction_addchdir.m4
      change is deliberately NOT carried: patching an m4/*.m4 input would
      leave it newer than the shipped aclocal.m4 and trigger an
      autotools regen cascade on extract; the generated configure is the
      only thing the build runs.
    - checks/006.command_li and checks/198.sysval are deleted (they
      misbehave under Fil-C; 1.4.21 no longer ships 198.sysval).
    - gnulib tests guarded with __FILC__: test-calloc-gnu, test-free,
      test-malloc-gnu, test-reallocarray, test-sigsegv-catch-segv1/2 and
      test-spawn-pipe.sh (skip the killed-iterator cases). The 1.4.21
      gnulib snapshot dropped test-explicit_bzero.c and
      test-realloc-gnu.c, so their guards were dropped, and
      test-posix_spawn-script.c keeps its unconditional early return.
      New for 1.4.21: test-calloc-posix, test-malloc-posix,
      test-realloc-posix and test-memset_explicit also get __FILC__
      guards (Fil-C panics on oversized allocations instead of returning
      NULL + ENOMEM, and memset_explicit's heap test memcmps freed
      memory after rebuilding a pointer from its integer address).
    - tests/test-c32ispunct.c: skip the U+E003A TAG COLON check under
      __FILC__. Fil-C's clang does not preinclude musl's stdc-predef.h
      (GCC auto-preincludes it), so __STDC_ISO_10646__ is invisible and
      gnulib's uchar.h does not treat wchar_t as UCS-4; c32ispunct then
      uses gnulib's ISO-C-style uc_is_punct table, which classifies the
      Cf format character U+E003A as punctuation, unlike musl's
      iswpunct, which is what gets used when __STDC_ISO_10646__ is
      defined (as on musl+GCC).
